  "scope": "# Fixed two-pass small-core NTP control\n\nThe bridge curriculum is closed negative. Return to the original broad-source\nprediction question with one missing control: fixed two-pass training in the\nqualified small-core family. Earlier full-gradient small-core NTP used sampled\ndepth, while the old fixed-depth success used a different, large core. Do not\nattribute differences across those studies to one factor.\n\nKeep the existing 2/2/2 backbone, natural-source mixture/order, tokenizer,\ninitializers, primitive FP32 attention, scaled FP16 body, optimizer, routing,\nbatch and ordinary next-token objective. Change only the externally specified\ntraining-depth table to exactly two forward and two backward passes for every\nwindow. No task curriculum, new objective, teacher, generated data or router.\nThe unused, zero-weight future head remains in training solely to match the\nqualified original NTP references. Count it; remove it for scoring. A lean\none-pass challenger remains required before a practical efficiency claim.\n\n## Bounded TUNE\n\nUse original random seed 4127, the original TUNE panel, the same three rates\n0.008/0.016/0.032 and 576 updates / 301,465,908 real targets. Every endpoint\nfinishes its original half-horizon cooldown. Reuse the already completed\none-pass NTP tuning opportunities and preserve their receipts; do not call\nthem new work. Both arms therefore receive the same three-rate selection\nopportunity. Edge winners remain unbracketed; no extra rates follow.\n\nBefore the bulk fit, stop each candidate after 121 actual registered updates.\nThose are counted scientific work, not hidden qualification. Check complete\nmodel/optimizer/RNG restoration and replicas. Capture its next complete\n512-window gradient and require independent upstream FP32 agreement within\n2% for all named families and 0.003 nats for objective components. The unchanged\nscientific engine already passed complete numerical, distributed, optimizer\nand recovery gates; this adds a bounded gate for the uniform depth assignment.\n\nMeasure exactly 100 real post-compilation updates from progress timestamps\nafter updates 20 and 120. Include changing runtime LR scalars, data, clipping,\noptimizer and logging. Recompute remaining feasibility before continuing any\ntrial. The original measured Poisson path supplies a conservative initial\nbound, with 50% step margin and explicit cold/probe/CPU tails. Whole-stage cap\n5,400 seconds, phase caps 1,200 seconds. Preserve 512GiB host memory, 35GiB disk\nand 10,000 free inodes. Ordinary failures cancel only owned workers.\n\nFinish all three cooled TUNE endpoints, score every one independently in FP32,\nselect minimum TUNE NLL (lower LR breaks ties), and require another complete\ngradient audit at the selected endpoint. No held-out exit selection: depth two\nis the only trained operating point. Repeated one-pass inference is unsupported\nand cannot establish a reusable-refinement claim.\n\n## Prespecified next scientific question\n\nTUNE is not a learning result or MAIN admission. A separately registered MAIN\nshould compare fixed two against one-pass NTP at 1B and 2B targets, plus exact\none-pass training-work endpoints. Both use independent complete cooldowns from\nstable prefixes, original random weights and equal source order. Run the\npredeclared exposures regardless of an early unfavorable mean if numerical and\nmeasured-budget gates pass. Keep the 0.01-nat useful-effect criterion. No claim\nthat the literature's token thresholds transfer to this lookup-heavy model.\n\nThe MAIN budget, full tables/counters, reused work, LR choices, prediction\ncost and primary readout must be frozen before launch. This document admits\nonly bounded TUNE after its explicit gates. Any promising discovery still\nneeds strong lean/cheap controls, fresh source groups and at least five fresh\npaired confirmation seeds under the unchanged sequential-alpha charter.\nNo TEST, reserve access, terminal alpha or terminal success here.\n",
